  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nThe cold front that brought showers and thunderstorms to the \nNorthland Friday evening is currently advancing out of the \nforecast area. Early this morning, only a few lingering showers \nremain behind the boundary. Winds are gradually turning out of \nthe northwest during the overnight hours, bringing gusts in the \n15 to 20 mph range. For today, expect a cooler and breezy day as\nhigh temperatures reach only into the 60s to lower 70s. \nNorthwest winds will increase by mid morning, with gusts rising \ninto the 20 to 25 mph range through the afternoon. Cyclonic flow\naloft will bring some stratocumulus clouds to the Borderlands, \nwhile most other areas will see only passing high clouds.\n\nQuiet and even cooler conditions settle in for Sunday as \nsurface high pressure slides in to settle over Minnesota \novernight. High temperatures on Sunday will generally stay in \nthe 60s under mostly sunny skies. As winds become light and \nvariable Sunday night under clear skies, temperatures will drop \nsignificantly. It's not the best night for radiational cooling, \nbut I anticipate overnight lows cooler than the median of \nguidance. Low temps should fall into the mid 30s to low 40s. The\ntypical cold spots could drop into the low 30s, which may \nsupport the development of patchy frost by early Monday morning.\n\nA strong upper low pressure system moves from the northern \nPlains across the southern Canadian Prairies and into northern \nOntario Monday through Tuesday of next week. This system will tap \ninto a plume of rich moisture, spreading another round of \nwidespread rain and scattered thunderstorms across the region from\nwest to east starting Monday afternoon and continues Monday \nnight. There is a risk for moderate to heavy soaking rainfall, \nwith a 60 to 80 percent probability of getting at least a half \ninch of rain by 7 AM Tuesday. Severe weather is not anticipated. \nShowers will linger into Tuesday before the system departs to the \neast. \n\nHigh pressure builds into the region beginning Tuesday night \nand Wednesday, allowing cooler air to filter into the Northland.\nHigh temperatures through the middle of the week will remain \ncomfortably in the 60s with overnight lows in the 40s. A \ngenerally quiet weather pattern takes hold for Thursday and \nFriday, though there are some chances for showers and isolated \nstorms. Temperatures will begin to slowly warm back into the 70s\nfor Friday and Saturday as southerly return flow sets up ahead \nof the next potential system.",
